dsv41 patch 0042b (19.10.2026): exl3_mrow for 17..32 rows, rewritten for speed. Replaces 0042 (apply one of the two):
same base, the recipe's plugin vllm_exl3_moe (cc59c36) with csrc/exl3_mrow.cu as in the image and dense.py incl. our
0035; the dense.py part is 0042's.
Why: with 0042, 18 / 24 rows still cost 1.23 / 1.31 x the 16-row time although 5 of 6 dense plans were unchanged (only
wo_b went from 80 to 160 items): the ~99 KB items ran one block per SM, transformed the whole input slice (and zeroed
rows m..31) before their weight stream got past the first 8 stages, and summed k-slices one dependent load at a time.
How: 1..16 rows keep the original kernel, plan, workspace and counters, unchanged in the source (same bits). 17..32 rows
run in a new mrow32_kernel<K, V> on plan g32, variant by EXL3_MROW32_VARIANT (read at registration, default ring):
  ring   the input is transformed per 128-wide k block inside the main loop (loads in flight under the mma, one
         barrier per block) into two 32 x 128 stages instead of the whole slice: ~27 KB of shared memory at K5, so the
         16-row plan's k-slices (no cap, wo_b back to 80 items) and two blocks per SM
  draft  0042 (reference)
  lean   0042's plan without the zero fill, 8 transform tasks in flight, 12 trellis stages, k-slice sum with its loads
         issued ahead
  ring1  ring with one block per SM, no register cap, 16 stages
17..32 rows are deterministic (k-slices summed in slice order) but sum in a different order than 0042 and the exllamav3
path, so texts under 3-4 streams can diverge from some token on (same distribution).
Measured (19.10., GB10, 2000 MHz; micro-benchmark of the 43 dense layers incl. the drafter, one rank, ms per round):
  rows             16      18      20      24
  draft (0042)  14.80   18.22   18.41   19.00
  lean          14.80   15.34   15.80   16.75
  ring          14.80   13.21   13.31   13.65   (16 rows through the ring kernel: 12.69)
Max. rel. error 1.7e-3 against the reference (draft, lean, ring). GPU unit tests passed (76). The first benchmark run
reported ring1 and ring with EXL3_MROW32_ITEMS=144 as wrong; that was a bug in the benchmark (an output buffer freed
after graph capture and handed to later variants), not in the kernels. A second benchmark and a matrix test (7 shapes
incl. the drafter's main_proj, 4 variants x item targets x k-slice caps x row counts) passed.
Serve (GB10, 2000 MHz, against the previous production with 0042): ring for 17..32 rows only: geometric mean +0.2 %.
Ring from 9 rows on (EXL3_MROW32_MIN_ROWS=9): geometric mean +2.35 %; code 3 / 4 streams +5.0 / +7.7 %, JSON
+5.1 / +3.4 %, prose +1.6 / +6.6 % (T = 1). Rows 9..16 (2-3-stream windows, short prompt chunks) then also take the
ring kernel, which sums in another order than the 16-row kernel, so T = 0 texts can differ (code, 1 stream, T = 0:
-2.1 %, which we attribute to a different text, not to slower rounds).
Knobs: EXL3_MROW32_MIN_ROWS (default 17; we use 9); for measurements EXL3_MROW32_KS (k-slice cap), EXL3_MROW32_ITEMS
(item target) and the compile flag -DMROW32_VARIANTS=4 (ring only, shorter JIT build). New queries: info32 (plan),
occupancy (blocks per SM, registers, spills).
Switch: VLLM_EXL3_MROW_MAX_ROWS=32 as in 0042 (default 16 = previous behaviour). New source hash: build exl3_mrow before
the first start (README, quick start step 4).

